const addIshikawaMicronesianSection = (slug, section) => {
  const article = window.deepArticles[slug];
  if (!article || (article.sections || []).some(existing => existing.id === section.id)) return;
  article.sections.push(section);
};

addIshikawaMicronesianSection("japanese-language-micronesia",{id:"administration",title:"Administrative language and local government",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-m4x7c writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T14:14:30-06:00 --><p>The incorporated Micronesian territories followed the common Japanese nationality principle; Micronesian identity remained regional and cultural rather than a separate citizenship.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Japanese allowed district offices, schools, shipping companies, hospitals, military services, and employers to exchange records across islands whose residents shared no single Indigenous language. Fluency made an islander's school certificate, referral, service record, pay account, and application legible beyond the home district.</p><p>Common records did not make island government uniform. Councils, chiefly and lineage authorities, churches, mission schools, and community associations continued to mediate land, welfare, worship, family obligations, local teaching, and communication with district officials. Preservation-era readers, broadcasts, dictionaries, interpreter services, and cultural classes could strengthen those bodies. Post-preservationist administration retained them where speakers and local institutions could sustain regular use, while Japanese remained the language of national mobility and inter-island public service.</p><p>The lingua franca originated in conquest, segregated colonial schools, settler privilege, military mobilization, land acquisition, and unequal access to secondary education. Islanders had to learn Japanese more often than settlers or officials had to learn island languages. Later common citizenship widened education and employment, yet accent and school background still marked origin and class. Administrative reach and cultural survival could improve together while strategic command and the prestige hierarchy remained Japanese.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"});

addIshikawaMicronesianSection("indigenous-government-japanese-micronesia",{id:"division",title:"Division of authority",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-n3k6p writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T14:18:30-06:00 --><p>Customary government remained effective where titleholders, lineages, councils, and mediators possessed the knowledge and daily compliance needed to settle land, reef access, adoption, inheritance, compensation, ceremony, and family obligation. District and municipal offices supplied salaried staff, written records, courts, police, schools, clinics, taxation, shipping connections, and public works. The balance differed among high islands, atolls, settler-majority centers, and remote communities.</p><p>Incorporation gave Micronesians Japanese citizenship while leaving Tokyo and its territorial commands in control of nationality, defense, criminal law, national standards, strategic ports, inter-island transport, major concessions, and final administrative review.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Customary authorities could decide many local questions because district government relied on their knowledge and legitimacy. They could not convert cultural recognition into a separate island citizenship or veto a national strategic decision.</p><p>Preservation-era grants, protected ceremonies, language programs, councils, and recognition of titles could strengthen local institutions. Post-preservationist government kept those arrangements when they maintained welfare, mediation, records, and peace. Official recognition could also select one claimant, freeze a changing custom, subordinate women or rival houses, and make cooperation with the district office a condition of authority. The arrangement preserved real island government inside a system founded on conquest, settler privilege, land acquisition, and unequal access to professional administration.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"});
